  "kind": "bit-matching-detail",
  "title": "The discount-security functions run a fixed formula order on the x87 unit",
  "statement": "With B the year basis and D the day count, Excel evaluates DISC = (1 - pr/red) * (B/D); INTRATE = ((red - inv)/inv) * (B/D); RECEIVED = inv / (1 - (disc*D)/B); PRICEDISC = red - ((disc*red)*D)/B; TBILLYIELD = ((100 - pr)/pr) * (360/D), each + - * / taken from the x87 unit at precision 64 and stored to binary64. Results can sit several units from the exact value: the DISC row below is 5 units below it.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "2,400 of 2,400 rows for each function with stored precision-64 arithmetic. Plain binary64 matches 2,398 to 2,399 of the same rows, so few rows separate the two; keeping intermediates in 80-bit registers without stores loses 475 to 1,335.",
  "inferred": null,
  "documentation": null,
